1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,000
Part 3

2
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:11,000
You will hear two theatre studies students discussing stage and screen performances of Shakespeare's play, Romeo and Juliet.

3
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:26,000
First, you have some time to look at questions 21 and 22.

4
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:34,000
Now listen carefully and answer questions 21 and 22.

5
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:39,000
Did you make notes while you were watching the performances of Romeo and Juliet, Gemma?

6
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:45,000
Yes, I did. I found it quite hard, though. I kept getting too involved in the play.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:51,000
Me too. I ended up not taking notes. I wrote down my impressions when I got home.

8
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:56,000
Do you mind if I check a few things with you in case I've missed anything?

9
00:00:56,000 --> 00:00:59,000
And I've also got some questions about our assignment.

10
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:04,000
No, it's good to talk things through. I may have missed things too.

11
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:11,000
OK, great. So first of all, I'm not sure how much information we should include in our reviews.

12
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:16,000
Right. Well, I don't think we need to describe what happens,

13
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:20,000
especially as Romeo and Juliet is one of Shakespeare's most well-known plays.

14
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:28,000
Yeah, everyone knows the story. In an essay, we'd focus on the poetry and Shakespeare's use of imagery, etc.

15
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:36,000
But that isn't really relevant in a review. We're supposed to focus on how effective this particular production is.

16
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:40,000
Hmm. We should say what made it a success or a failure.

17
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:46,000
And part of that means talking about the emotional impact the performance had on us. I think that's important.

18
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:53,000
Yes. And we should definitely mention how well the director handled important bits of the play,

19
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:57,000
like when Romeo climbs onto Juliet's balcony.

20
00:01:57,000 --> 00:01:59,000
And the fight between Mercutio and Tybalt.

21
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:06,000
Yes. It would also be interesting to mention the theatre space and how the director used it,

22
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:09,000
but I don't think we'll have space in 800 words.

23
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:13,000
No, OK. That all sounds quite straightforward.

24
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:24,000
Before you hear the rest of the discussion, you have some time to look at questions 23 to 30.

25
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:21,000
So, what about the Emporium Theatre's production of the play?

26
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:26,000
I thought some things worked really well, but there were some problems too.

27
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:29,000
Yeah. What about the set, for example?

28
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:36,000
I think it was visually really stunning. I'd say that was probably the most memorable thing about this production.

29
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:40,000
You're right. The set design was really amazing.

30
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:44,000
But actually, I have seen similar ideas used in other productions.

31
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:51,000
Hmm. What about the lighting? Some of the scenes were so dimly lit, it was quite hard to see.

32
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:56,000
I didn't dislike it. It helped to change the mood of the quieter scenes.

33
00:03:56,000 --> 00:03:58,000
That's a good point.

34
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:00,000
What did you think of the costumes?

35
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:04,000
I was a bit surprised by the contemporary dress, I must say.

36
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:06,000
Yeah. I think it worked well.

37
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:09,000
But I'd assumed it would be more conventional.

38
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:15,000
Me too. I liked the music at the beginning, and I thought the musicians were brilliant.

39
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:21,000
But I thought they were wasted because the music didn't have much impact in Acts 2 and 3.

40
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:23,000
Yes, that was a shame.

41
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:28,000
One problem with this production was that the actors didn't deliver the lines that well.

42
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:30,000
They were speaking too fast.

43
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:32,000
It was a problem, I agree.

44
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:38,000
But I thought it was because they weren't speaking loudly enough, especially at key points in the play.

45
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:41,000
I actually didn't have a problem with that.

46
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:46,000
It's been an interesting experience watching different versions of Romeo and Juliet, hasn't it?

47
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:51,000
Definitely. It's made me realise how relevant the play still is.

48
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:56,000
Right. I mean, a lot's changed since Shakespeare's time, but in many ways nothing's changed.

49
00:04:56,000 --> 00:05:00,000
There are always disagreements and tension between teenagers and their parents.

50
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:04,000
Yes. That's something all young people can relate to.

51
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:08,000
More than the violence and the extreme emotions in the play.

52
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:11,000
How did you find watching it in translation?

53
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:13,000
Really interesting.

54
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:18,000
I expected to find it more challenging, but I could follow the story pretty well.

55
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:21,000
I stopped worrying about not being able to understand all the words

56
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:25,000
and focused on the actors' expressions. The ending was pretty powerful.

57
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:30,000
Yes. That somehow intensified the emotion for me.

58
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:35,000
Did you know Shakespeare's been translated into more languages than any other writer?

59
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:40,000
Hmm. What's the reason for his international appeal, do you think?

60
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:44,000
I was reading that it's because his plays are about basic themes

61
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:46,000
that people everywhere are familiar with.

62
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:50,000
Yeah. And they can also be understood on different levels.

63
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:52,000
The characters have such depth.

64
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:57,000
Right. Which allows directors to experiment and find new angles.

65
00:05:57,000 --> 00:05:59,000
That's really important because...

66
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:05,000
That is the end of Part 3.

67
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:09,000
You now have half a minute to check your answers to Part 3.

68
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:18,000
Thank you.

